Well, hmm.   Spoilers may follow: 

First of all, the food issue.

FOOD:
In Nethack, food is often a problem for beginners.  There are three ways
to feed yourself that I know of.  The best way is by finding food items,
such as food rations, lembas wafers, various fruits, etc, and eating them.
However, it is difficult to find enough of those.  So, the next alternative
is eating corpses.  Most creatures have edible corpses.  Some corpses have
side effects, the most common being poison.  Figure out which corpses are
poisonous and don't eat them unless you're poison resistant.  Finally, the
third option is prayer.  Being weak or fainting from hunger is usually cause
for your god to aid you.  If you haven't prayed recently, try waiting until
you are weak or fainting, then #pray.  This will often fill your stomach.
If all these fail, try reducing your food consumption.  Wearing rings or
amulets increase the rate at which you get hungry, as does regeneration.
Casting spells uses up a certain amount of food, also.  Finally, try playing
a class that starts out with food.  Off the top of my head, Valkryies start
off with one or two, Archeologists have about four or five, and tourists have
huge amounts.  Barbarians *might* also, I don't recall.  

The other thing to worry about is monsters.

BEGINNING MONSTERS:

The most commonly encountered critters in the top layers of the dungeon
are floating eyes, kobolds (various sizes), gnomes (various types), hobbits,
dwarves (various types), hobgoblins, orcs, goblins, bats, acid blobs, 
gray oozes, newts and other lizards, and molds and fungi (various types).
Here's a quick runthrough of these.

Floating eyes:  Small, floating eyes that drift aimlessly around the dungeon.
                Their gaze can paralyze you if you touch them, so beware!  
                However, they have no other attack form, and can not do any
                damage, so the best way to attack is to throw things at them.
                Throwing does not touch them, so they cannot paralyze you and
                can be easily dispatched.  Floating eyes only tend to pose a
                problem if you carelessly run into them, and there is another
                monster around, or if you have a low number of things to throw
                at them.  Their corpses grant a useful intrinsic, ESP.

Kobolds:  Short (except for large kobolds, of course) humanoids.  Often have a
          supply of darts, which they throw at unsuspecting adventurers such
          as yourself.  Pose no real danger to a well-armed fighter, if you
          are not severely wounded.  Occasionally you may meet a kobold shaman.
          These are not especially dangerous, but can cast spells which drain 
          your luck or stun you.  Their corpses are poisonous.

Gnomes:  Gnomes are small, neutral creatures.  They have a mine set up
         in the dungeon, where they work with dwarves to pluck gems from the
         earth.  It is rumored there are secrets at the bottom of the mines.
         They are often peaceful to neutrally-aligned characters, but pose 
         no threat to most adventurers, much as kobolds do.  However, gnome
         kings and gnomish wizards can be more difficult, and low-level people
         should treat them with caution.  

Hobbits:  Hobbits are like those described in the works of Tolkien.  They
          are lawfully-aligned, and are often peaceful to lawful folk.  They
          are often armed with slings, or runed (elvish) daggers.

Dwarves:  These are short, bearded humanoids.  They are quite nasty to fight
          for beginning characters, and are often well-armored and armed, so
          they can be a good source of equipment.  They are lawfully aligned,
          and will often be peaceful to lawfuls.  The main thing of note with
          dwarves is that they may carry a pick-axe, which is useful for 
          tunneling.  Dwarves are ruled by dwarf lords and dwarf kings, both
          of whom can be quite dangerous to inexperienced adventurers.

Goblinkind (Hobgoblins, orcs, goblins, etc):  The goblinkind are a vicious,
           chaotic group of brutish humanoids.  They constantly lust after
           gold, and wear armor and use weapons extensively.  Despite their
           nasty tendencies, orcs will group together in large packs, making
           them dangerous.  Hobgoblins and goblins will often be peaceful
           to chaotics.

Bats:  The only mammal which can fly, bats tend to be easy to kill, and quite 
       harmless.  Occasionally peaceful if you are neutral.  There are other
       types of bats, such as giant and vampire bats, later in the dungeon.
       Bat corpses will stun you if eaten.  Avoid unless absolutely necessary.

Acid blobs:  These are small, oozing blobs of acid.  Like floating eyes, their
             only method of damaging you (splash of acid) only works if you
             strike them.  Use the same technique as with a floating eye.  
             Occasionally a blob will be peaceful if you are neutral.
             Their corpses are acidic, but edible.

Gray oozes:  Probably relatives of the acid blob, these are greyish, flat
             oozing creatures.  They can attack you, unlike acid blobs, 
             but do average damage.  They have no acid backlash, unlike acid
             blobs.  Their corpses are acidic.  

Newts and other Lizards:  Small lizards.   Stupid, generally easy to kill.
                          Note that lizard (not newts or geckos, just lizards)
                          corpses do not rot.

Molds and Fungi:  Like the fuzz under your toilet, only more vicious.  There
                  are too many of these to describe here, but here are some
                  general tips.  Almost all are stationary, and the best way to
                  avoid their attack form (which varies with color) is to
                  attack them with hurled objects.  They tend to give a
                  intrinsic resistance to whatever attack form they use.  Many
                  are poisonous, but may grant poison resistance.  Also, a good
                  number are hallucinogenic.  

Finally, some quick tactics and playing strategy:

Drink from all fountains on early levels.  Occasionally you may get 
something nice.  If you're being harassed by creatures, enscribing the
mystic word Elbereth upon the floor may give you some breathing room.
Priests and Samurai are good at kicking.  Try it.  Chests may be
kicked open, or #forced open.  Be careful not to break your weapon
if you try and force a chest!  Potions are usually safe to drink,
although many have harmful side effects (but they probably won't kill
you).  Scrolls should be read carefully.  Many are nasty.  Rings should
not be worn if they're cursed.  How do you know if they're cursed?  Pets
won't step on cursed items.  Engraving with a wand often gives you a clue
as to its function.  Or, zap a monster (but be watchful for beneficial wands)
Finally, if you're having problems figuring out the game, try playing in
discover mode for a bit.  Nethack -X to enter.

